{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED for exact head 91990a2615827336db51035cd0989c319ab1556a, against primary artifact c761a8d7 at cmd/gs and the live candidate artifacts c526e6a4 at internal/app, c796dd0b at internal/workroom and 7a6b9ba2 at docs.\n\nTwo of the three carried-over findings are genuinely closed, and I confirmed both by mutation rather than by reading. The third is not closed, and I found two further defects the earlier reviews did not reach. Every claim below was run against the exact head; nothing is reasoned-only, and the reviewed worktree was never touched (all mutation ran in a throwaway `git worktree add --detach` copy, since removed).\n\nWHAT IS CLOSED.\n\nFinding 1 (unrelated unmerged candidates) is fixed and covered. successionPredecessors admits an artifact only when its commit is an ancestor of the merge's first parent, or is the exact reviewed candidate. Mutating the ancestor test to admit everything makes TestMergeLeavesAnUnrelatedCandidateArtifactLive fail with \"merge retired an unrelated unmerged candidate artifact\"; the neighbouring merge-guard test still passes, so the test is discriminating rather than broadly coupled.\n\nFinding 3 (invalid generated paths) is fixed and covered. Replacing preflightSuccession's guard body with `_ = path` makes TestMergePreflightRefusesInvalidGeneratedArtifactPaths fail immediately.\n\nThe fold seams are also covered. Disabling the state@1 artifact-path rule fails both admission tests; widening hasAuthorizedMergeReceipt to accept any target fails TestMergeReceiptDoesNotAuthorizeAnUnrelatedCrossAuthorRetirement while the three neighbouring receipt tests keep passing.\n\nFINDING A, BLOCKING. `gs merge` cannot merge this head into main, and cannot merge anything else into a documented area either. Its own citation preflight refuses first, and there is no override.\n\npreflightSuccession calls RefuseCitedRetirementInCheckout with allowed hard-coded false for every retirement target, and mergeCommand exposes no --cited-ok (the flag exists on `gs supersede` at main.go:1023 and on `gs batch` at 1138, not on merge).\n\nMeasured, not read. I replayed the real live artifact set from the resident projection and the real first-parent diff of this head against main 8924b4d0 through successionPredecessors, planSuccession and preflightSuccession, with the tracked tree of the head as the checkout. The plan is publish [cmd/gs docs internal/app internal/wireparity internal/workroom ui] with 20 retirements, and the preflight returns:\n\n  retiring git:sha1:...#git:sha1:fcf3a656... would leave 11 documentation page(s) resting on a withdrawn pointer:\n    docs/README.md, docs/anchoring.md, docs/concepts/components.md, docs/concepts/staleness.md,\n    docs/how-to/end-to-end.md, docs/reference/architecture.md, docs/reference/gs/merge.md,\n    docs/reference/gs/status.md, docs/reference/gs/supersede.md, docs/reference/mcp/supersede.md, docs/why.md\n\nso HEAD never moves. Two more predecessors in the same plan are cited the same way: 4eeb3acf at cmd/gs by 12 pages and 5676cc5c at internal/app by 4.\n\nThis is structural, not an accident of today's log. docs/anchoring.md says rests_on names implementation artifacts, and ClassifyCitation (internal/docset/docset.go:326) makes a retired cited basis FATAL for the docs gate. So the set is required to cite exactly the artifacts a merge must retire; the guard then refuses that retirement; and the guard's own advice — \"repoint them at the successor in the same head, then retire\" — is unsatisfiable here, because the successor event does not exist until after the merge that is being refused. Automating retirement without also carrying the citations forward leaves no legal move: refuse (today's behaviour, merging stops) or bypass (main goes red on the docs gate).\n\nI do not think the fix is simply to pass allowed=true. Something has to close the loop — a --cited-ok on merge with the flare accepted deliberately, a post-merge repointing step the command performs or names, or a docset rule that follows a retirement to its recorded successor. That is a design decision for the requester, not something I should choose in a review, but the head cannot land as it stands: the first thing it does after landing is stop the next merge.\n\nFINDING B, BLOCKING, CONDITION NOT MET. The wider-path rule is still not mutation-sensitive. The conditions require it, and the ready-for-review report 93b9c8f5 states it was fixed by making the fixture order-sensitive.\n\nI replaced widerPath's body with `return false` and ran the whole cmd/gs package: ok, 125s, nothing failed — not TestMergeWiderPathWinsOverNestedArtifact, not any other test. Reordering the fixture did not change the masking hugh identified, because the mask is not about list order. In widest(), the fallback is the changed path itself, and the narrow artifact's path IS that string, so after the narrow artifact is considered `winner == fallback` is still true, and the wider artifact is then accepted by that arm whatever widerPath returns. A fixture that actually exercises the comparison needs the narrow artifact at a path that differs from the fallback — for example a change to `internal/workroom/fold.go` with live artifacts at `internal/workroom` and at some other covering directory — or widest() needs a fallback that no artifact can equal.\n\nFINDING C, BLOCKING, SECURITY. The new cross-author authority is not bound to a merge. Any participant can retire any artifact in the log.\n\nvalidateMergeReceiptNow requires the receipt to cite a ratified, effective approval whose head equals merge_candidate, and whose named artifact is effective, cited by the approval, and authored by someone other than the approver. It requires nothing of the receipt's own author, and merge_head and merge_target_pre_head are unverifiable strings the signer chooses. The retirement set is read straight out of body.merge_retirements, which the same signer wrote. hasAuthorizedMergeReceipt then only checks that the supersession cites its own receipt and that the target appears in that plan — and a plan entry with an empty successor skips citesMergeSuccessor entirely.\n\nRun, not reasoned. In a fold-level probe, `agent` (a bare participant, not a ratifier, with no part in the approval and no merge) minted an assert citing the public ratified approval with merge_retirements {\"victim\":\"\"}, then superseded an artifact authored by `operator`. Verdict: effective, \"merge approval authorized artifact succession\"; the victim artifact is retired. Before this head that required the `ratifier` role.\n\nThe consequence is not theoretical vandalism only: a retired artifact makes `gs merge` refuse and takes the docs gate red, so this is a one-signature denial-of-merge available to every participant. The docs claim (\"the narrow cross-author exception applies only when the supersession cites a receipt signed by the same actor and that receipt carries the ratified independent approval chain\") is literally true and still describes an unbounded authority, and architecture.md's \"deterministic cross-author artifact succession\" reads tighter than what the fold verifies.\n\nSome bound the fold can actually check would help: require at least one published successor artifact resting on the receipt, and require every retired target's path to be equal to or under one of the receipt's successor paths, with deletions carried in a separate signed list; or require the receipt's author to hold a role. Whatever is chosen, the page should state the real bound.\n\nNON-BLOCKING NOTES.\n\nplanSuccession's mergeHead parameter and publishedByThisMerge are dead in production: main.go:502 always passes \"\", and the resume path uses the sealed plan through recordedSuccessionPlan rather than replanning. Only tests pass a real merge head, so TestMergeRetryRecognizesItsAlreadyPublishedSuccessor guards a path the command cannot take. Either wire it or drop it.\n\nexistingGitMergeReceipt now returns an error whenever any commit matching the approval trailer fails readMergeReceipt, and readMergeReceipt requires the two new trailers. Merge commits already in main have no Gitseq-Retirements, so a repeat invocation against an already-used old approval reports \"malformed merge receipt commit\" instead of the intended \"already used\" refusal. Fails closed, but the message misleads.\n\nMixed-version hazard. Decode gained state@1 and ProfileVersion advanced to workroom-fold@3, but a binary built before this head has no case for state@1, so a resident or MCP adapter still running from main cannot interpret records written after the merge. Whoever lands this must restart every resident and adapter at the new commit; a line saying so in merge.md would be cheap.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layers are cmd/gs (surface and composition), internal/app (application host and admission boundary), internal/workroom (Workroom interpreter and fold) and docs. The head changes three contracts: gs merge now records succession itself, the fold admits a new class of cross-author supersession, and the state schema is versioned to state@1 with fold@3. docs/reference/architecture.md and docs/reference/gs/merge.md are updated in the same head and candidate artifacts are published at that head, so the AGENTS.md requirement is met in form. One correction is needed in substance: the architecture entry describing the receipt authority as deterministic cross-author succession overstates what the fold checks (Finding C).\n\nSECURITY. Finding C is an unresolved defect and blocks on its own. The rest of the boundary work is sound: preflight runs before HEAD moves (--no-ff --no-commit, plan, preflight, then commit) and a refusal aborts the tentative merge and deletes the receipt reservation; the runBatch citation bypass is deliberately authorized by that exact-checkout preflight and says so; intent.Verify now runs before submission rather than after; the state@0 admission gate closes the raw-schema evasion, keyed on the governed RenderArtifact class rather than the kind name; the sealed Git plan means a crash between the merge commit and the first durable act cannot silently widen the retirement set, and TestMergeRetryBeforeDurableReceiptUsesTheSealedGitPlan proves the post-merge artifact is not swept in.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Nothing large. Beyond the dead mergeHead parameter above: errorsNewMalformedDiff is a one-line helper whose name imitates a package function and is used twice — inline it or name it malformedDiffError; mergeReceiptPlan carries three fallbacks (the parsed copy, then the canonical record) where the append site already writes both, so one lookup would do; and staleness() recomputes f.mergeReceiptPlan(\u0026record) inside the per-basis loop when it could be hoisted once per record.\n\nGATES RUN AT THIS EXACT HEAD, in the reviewed worktree at 91990a26, clean: go vet ./... clean; go build ./... clean; go test ./... -count=1 fully green, all 24 packages ok including internal/docset (474s) and cmd/gs (256s). Honest note: an earlier full run under load had internal/docset time out at 603s; the clean re-run is the one I report. No ui/ file changed at this head, so no tsc or UI suite was run. git merge-tree --write-tree against main 8924b4d0 is clean, and the head is pushed at origin/request/merge-driven-succession. All mutations and both probes ran in a detached throwaway worktree that has been removed; the reviewed worktree is clean and still at the exact head.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:c761a8d7964e7dc35b6258cb32087540c0e0e75e","head":"91990a2615827336db51035cd0989c319ab1556a","verdict":"changes-requested"}}